'''Portugis''', or '''Ternateño''', was a [[Portuguese-based creole language]] spoken by [[Christians]] of mixed [[Portuguese people|Portuguese]] and [[Malay people|Malay]] ancestry in the islands of [[Ambon Island|Ambon]] and [[Ternate]] in the [[Moluccas]] ([[Indonesia]]), from the 16th to the middle of the 20th century.

Portugis was a [[creole language|creole]] based chiefly on [[Portuguese language|Portuguese]] and [[Malay language|Malay]].<ref>{{cite web| url = https://archive.today/20120729041842/http://www.colonialvoyage.com/pt/asia/lingua/heranca.html#selection-1425.1-1425.277 | title = A Herança da Língua Portuguesa no Oriente (Ásia) | publisher = | access-date = 16 February 2014 | language = pt }}</ref>

The language was gradually replaced by a variant of Malay called [[Ambonese Malay]].

